  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/0/0b/Amazing_Spider-Man_Vol_1_12.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Peter drops by the Daily Bugle and notes that Betty has returned as Jameson's secretary.&nbsp; The Bugle accuses Spider-Man of allowing Dr. Octopus to escape in Philadelphia (last issue).&nbsp; Now Ock is on a crime spree \"thruout [sic] the nation.\"&nbsp; He's <i>trying</i> to goad Spider-Man into attacking him, <i>sure</i> that he's more powerful.&nbsp; But he doesn't immediately come after him, so Ock decides to return to NYC and find <i>him</i> instead!&nbsp; But Peter is home and actually seems to be catching a cold.\n\nAt the Bugle, Betty answers the phone, not realizing that it's Ock, only calling to ensure she's there.&nbsp; He'll use <i>her</i> as bait for Spider-Man.&nbsp; And indeed he <i>does</i> arrive for her, just after Parker comes to talk Betty home.&nbsp; Ock demands that Jameson print a note in the paper, telling Spider-Man to meet him at Coney Island!&nbsp; If he wants the girl, he must come <i>alone</i>, but he'll allow one photographer to take pictures of the defeat.&nbsp; Jameson turns to Parker and tags him for the job!\n\nPeter is indeed getting woozy from the oncoming illness, and it's affecting his ability to cling to the walls, but nothing will stop him from getting to Coney Island!&nbsp; But he's obviously feeling very weak, but he lunges at Ock, trying to throw a punch, but the blow is barely felt by his opponent, who thinks it's a trick!&nbsp; Ock is easily able to handle the weakened Spider-Man and even says \"You're like a human punching bag!!&nbsp; What's <i>happened</i> to you?\"&nbsp; and \"He doesn't even struggle as I try to remove his mask!&nbsp; There can only be <i>one </i>answer...\"\n\nAnd he rips off Spider-Man's mask!&nbsp; \"I should have <i>known</i>!!&nbsp; It <i>isn't</i> Spider-Man!&nbsp; It's that weakling brat, Peter Parker!\"&nbsp; The onlookers are amazed!&nbsp; Peter could have been killed!&nbsp; The police get him home, where hours later, Aunt May has summoned the doctor, who assures her that it's only the \"twenty-four hour virus\" and that he'll be fine by morning.\n\nAnd sure enough, when he wakes up, he's good as new.&nbsp; (<i>Great panel on the bottom right of page 9</i>!)&nbsp; At school, his classmates have all heard that he impersonated Spider-Man to try and rescue Betty, for which Liz thinks he's wonderful, but Flash is sure Peter only did it because he was sure he wouldn't actually run into Ock.&nbsp; Liz tells him off!\n\nMeanwhile, Ock is furious that the papers are saying he was fooled by a teenager and allowed his hostage to go free even <i>without</i> having to fight Spider-Man!&nbsp; He releases the large animals from the zoo to terrorize the city.&nbsp; That occupies the police and Spider-Man while he continues his rampage.&nbsp; At one point (page 14), he dislodges a sign on the side of the building with the word \"LEEDIT Inc\" (clever, obviously short for \"Lee\" and \"Dit\"ko - heh - I suppose it could have also been \"LEEDIT Co.\" short for \"company\" but perhaps that might have been a little <i>too</i> blatant!).&nbsp; Spider-Man finally catches up with him, and the battle begins!\n\nPages of battle action ensue, with Spider-Man pointing out \"You're not fighting a weak <i>imitation</i> of Spider-Man now...this time you're facing the <i>real thing</i>!\"&nbsp; Eventually the two crash into a \"deserted sculptor's studio\" where some spilled cleaning fluid starts a fire (Ock points out that \"this building is deserted\" - man, I'm now wondering whether NYC is littered with abandoned buildings - <i>wow</i>).&nbsp; Spider-Man points out that they both need to get out of there, but Ock is livid enough to pay no heed and proceeds to get stuck under a fallen sculpture while Spider-Man makes a web shield to protect him against the flames and makes it to an open window and escapes.&nbsp; Soon the fire department arrives and drags the weakened Doc Ock out into the hands of the waiting police.\n\nOn the street below, Peter Parker now watches and turns down a request from Liz Allen (apparently misspelled in this issue - actually Allan) to attend a party, but he's got a date with Betty - she can have Flash instead!&nbsp; Liz realizes that she probably deserves that treatment after the way they've treated him.",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "\"average\" is about the word to use here.&nbsp; The novelty of seeing Peter unmasked by an enemy was sure to sell copies of the book, and indeed it wasn't a trick (sort of) - but while Ock does indeed remove the mask, he thinks <i>he's</i> the one being tricked.&nbsp; The gimmick of having Peter be too sick to fight Ock felt sort of like a trick, but okay, it could happen...\n\nThe supporting characters are mostly relegated to the back seat in this story, as the action takes center stage, with the one possible ongoing change being the attitude that Liz Allan has of him after realizing that he put himself in danger and \"posed\" as Spider-Man to try and rescue Betty.&nbsp; Is this a one-issue attitude adjustment, or is she really seeing Peter in another light? - we'll stay tuned!\n\nAs for Doc Ock, the action sequences were good but otherwise not really noteworthy.&nbsp; Spider-Man catches the villain, he is captured, and life goes on.&nbsp; It was, in the end, only an average issue with no notable <i>faults</i>, mind you, but not life-altering in any way and probably soon to be forgotten about.\n\nI loved seeing the \"LEEDIT Inc\" sign, as I pointed out above, and also the cover blurb of \"Not a Dream!&nbsp; Not an Imaginary Tale!\" - obviously in response to DC's common practice of the time.&nbsp; Note that the cover of <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> #121 (an 8.0 copy of which I have displayed in my office) has a similar blurb, which is probably why I immediately took note of it on the cover of <i>this</i> issue!\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n<b>FIXME</b>",
  "side_note": [
    "The <b>Special Announcements Section</b> of this issue [LINK:1964:ASM_12|ASM #12|2027-06-02] explains that Betty is actually <i>younger</i> than Peter, having had to leave school and get a job because she needed the money, intending to complete her education when she gets a chance.&nbsp; By the way, that announcement goes on to declare Peter as a \"high school senior.\")"
  ],
  "letters_page": "The \"Spider's Web\" page contains a letter from future comic book creator Dave Cockrum.",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
